<h2>First identify the statute, then the remedy</h2>
<p>The commonest error in this field is treating "maintenance" as a single jurisdiction. It is not. The forum that passed the order and the statute it acted under together fix the appellate route, and the routes are not interchangeable. A revision filed where an appeal lay, or an appeal filed against an order that is only revisable, will be returned or dismissed — usually after limitation has run.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Order</th><th>Forum that passed it</th><th>Route of challenge</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Final order under Section 144 BNSS (125 CrPC)</td><td>Family Court / Magistrate</td><td>Criminal revision to the High Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim order under Section 144 BNSS</td><td>Family Court / Magistrate</td><td>Article 227 petition; revision generally resisted as interlocutory</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Maintenance pendente lite, Section 24 HMA</td><td>Family Court</td><td>Appeal under Section 19(1), Family Courts Act (Delhi view)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Permanent alimony, Section 25 HMA</td><td>Family Court</td><td>Appeal under Section 19, Family Courts Act</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monetary relief / interim relief under DV Act</td><td>Magistrate</td><td>Appeal under Section 29 to the Court of Session, within 30 days</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 18 HAMA maintenance decree</td><td>Family Court / Civil Court</td><td>First appeal as from a decree</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What a revisional court will and will not do</h2>
<p>Revision against a Section 144 BNSS order is supervisory. The High Court asks whether the order is legal, regular and proper — not whether it would itself have fixed a different figure. Interference typically follows where the trial court ignored the affidavit of assets, attributed income without material, overlooked a disqualification pleaded and proved, granted maintenance from a date without reasons, or computed arrears wrongly. A mere quarrel with quantum, without perversity, rarely succeeds.</p>

<h2>The special problem of interim orders</h2>
<p>Interim maintenance is meant to be quick and tentative, so the system discourages appellate battles over it. Two safety valves exist. First, the court that passed the interim order can itself vary it on a change of circumstances — a job loss, a remarriage, fresh disclosure of income. Second, the High Court under Article 227 corrects jurisdictional excess: unreasoned orders, amounts fixed contrary to the affidavits, or refusal to decide the interim application at all. Petitioners should come with clean hands; a challenger in arrears is usually put to terms before being heard.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Weak challenge</h3><p>"The amount is too high." No compliance, no deposit, quantum-only grievance, affidavit incomplete.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>Strong challenge</h3><p>Order ignores documented income of the claimant, attributes income without any material, or awards double recovery across overlapping orders — with compliance up to date.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Points that decide these challenges in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Compliance first.</strong> Courts routinely decline to hear defaulters. Paying under protest preserves both credibility and the challenge.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The affidavits are the record.</strong> Most reversals trace back to what the affidavits of assets and income did or did not show. A challenge is only as strong as the disclosure supporting it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Set-off across statutes.</strong> Where maintenance runs under two orders — say DV Act and Section 144 BNSS — the challenge should seek adjustment, not annihilation; courts protect against double recovery but will not leave the claimant remediless.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Limitation.</strong> The DV Act appeal carries a thirty-day period; revisions and Article 227 petitions, though more flexible, punish stale challenges. Diarise from the date of the order.</div>
</div>

<div class="note"><p>For a wife aggrieved by an inadequate award, the same routes apply in reverse — and enhancement petitions succeed most often where the trial court accepted the husband's pleaded income at face value despite lifestyle evidence to the contrary.</p></div>

<h2>Takeaway</h2>
<p>Challenging a maintenance order is a targeted exercise: identify the statute, pick the corresponding remedy, comply while you challenge, and attack findings rather than outcomes. The scattergun approach — filing everything everywhere — signals weakness and invites costs. This article is general information on practice in the Delhi courts and is not legal advice on any individual matter.</p>
